proof.text:
Write $
u:\operatorname{Spec}C^{
u}	o\operatorname{Spec}C$ for normalization. It is finite for the following explicit reason. The quotient $C_{\mathrm{red}}$ is Noetherian by finite-type Noetherianity (a quotient is generated as an algebra by its unit). Apply the affine-chart description to $(\operatorname{Spec}C)_{\mathrm{red}}$: normalization does not change on removing nilpotents by its componentwise definition. Thus $C^{
u}=\prod_{j=1}^r\overline{C/\mathfrak q_j}$, where $\mathfrak q_j$ are the finitely many minimal primes and the bars are integral closures in the respective fraction fields. Each domain $C/\mathfrak q_j$ is finite type over the Nagata ring $\widehat A$, hence N-2 by the universally-Japanese theorem. Taking the degree-one extension of its fraction field makes each displayed integral closure a finite $C/\mathfrak q_j$-module. Its finitely many generators also generate over $C$, and the union of these generators, inserted in the factors, generates their finite product. Thus $C^{
u}$ is finite over $C$. By the normalization-properties node it is normal and $C	o C^{
u}$ is an isomorphism over the assumed normal open $\operatorname{Spec}C\setminus V(IC)$.

Choose generators $f_1,\ldots,f_t$ of $I$; they exist because $B$ is Noetherian. On each $D(f_i)$ the map $C_{f_i}	o(C^{
u})_{f_i}$ is an isomorphism. Form the finite-algebra gluing datum with prime algebra $E^{\prime}=C^{
u}$, chart algebra $E_i=B_{f_i}$, transition $(C^{
u})_{f_i}\cong C_{f_i}=B_{f_i}\otimes_B C$, and the usual identifications $(B_{f_i})_{f_j}\cong(B_{f_j})_{f_i}$. The overlap and cocycle identities are the equalities $x/(f_i^a f_j^b)=x/(f_j^b f_i^a)$ and their three-denominator versions; the prime transitions are the inverses of the same structural isomorphism $C	o C^{
u}$ after localization, so satisfy those identities too. The hypotheses for finite-algebra formal gluing are now verified: $B	o C$ is flat, $B/I\cong C/IC$, $C^{
u}$ is finite, and each chart algebra is generated as a module by $1$. It gives a finite $B$-algebra $D$ and compatible algebra isomorphisms $D\otimes_B C\cong C^{
u}$ and $D_{f_i}\cong B_{f_i}$. Hence $\operatorname{Spec}D	o\operatorname{Spec}B$ is finite and is an isomorphism away from $V(I)$.
